##fileformat=VCFv4.3
##contig=<ID=chr1,length=1>
##contig=<ID=chr1,length=999>
##Authority=hts-specs' own test/vcf/{4.1,4.2}/passed/passed_meta_contig.vcf fixture declares ##contig=<ID=1> then ##contig=<ID=1,length=123456> and is expected to parse successfully -- direct precedent that a duplicate declaration ID is not a structural violation in the spec's own conformance suite (see DUPLICATE_METADATA_ID_HANDLING.md's "Why this warns instead of throwing" section).
##Authority=bcftools 1.23.1 (htslib 1.23.1) keeps the first ##contig=<ID=chr1,length=1> declaration and silently drops the duplicate, exit 0, no warning -- BcftoolsDependencyTest#testDuplicateContigIdBcftoolsKeepsFirst pins this live measurement; re-confirmed directly via bcftools view -h on 2026-08-16.
##Authority=vcf_validator 0.10.2 reports this file valid -- measured live via vcf_validator -i <file> -r summary,text on 2026-08-16.
##ExpectWarnContains=keeping the first declaration
##RoundTripDivergenceReason=VcfMetadata.Builder.addContig() drops the second, duplicate ##contig declaration when it detects the shared ID (DUPLICATE_METADATA_ID_HANDLING.md's reference table); the writer then emits only the surviving first declaration, so the round trip is not byte-identical even though it is semantically equivalent -- verified live during authoring: only the second ##contig line is missing from the written output.
